      "target": "prop-sec-front-matter-01.md",
      "narrative": null,
      "content": "By placing a movable mask in front of a double-slit to control the transmission through the individual slits, probability distributions for single- and double-slit arrangements were observed.",
      "truncated": false,
      "provenance": "ingested",
      "summary": null
    },
    {
      "id": "library/arxiv-1210.6243v1/blocks/prose-sec-front-matter",
      "types": [
        "folio-assistant-core:Prose",
        "doco:Section"
      ],
      "kind": "prose",
      "title": "Front matter",
      "pageStart": 1,
      "pageEnd": 2,
      "target": "../sections/sec-front-matter.md",
      "narrative": null,
      "content": "arXiv:1210.6243v1  [quant-ph]  22 Oct 2012\nControlled double-slit electron diﬀraction\nRoger Bach1, Damian Pope2, Sy-Hwang Liou1 and\nHerman Batelaan1\n1 Department of Physics and Astronomy, University of Nebraska-Lincoln, Theodore\nP. Jorgensen Hall, Lincoln, NE 68588, USA.\n2 Perimeter Institute for Theoretical Physics, 31 Caroline ST N Waterloo, Ontario,\nCanada N2L2Y5.\nE-mail: hbatelaan@unl.edu\nAbstract.\nDouble-slit diﬀraction is a corner stone of quantum mechanics.\nIt\nillustrates key features of quantum mechanics:\ninterference and the particle-wave\nduality of matter.\nIn 1965, Richard Feynman pr",
      "truncated": true,
      "provenance": "ingested",
      "summary": {
        "status": "not-summarised"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "library/arxiv-1210.6243v1/blocks/prose-sec-000",
      "types": [
        "folio-assistant-core:Prose",
        "doco:Section"
      ],
      "kind": "prose",
      "title": "Introduction",
      "pageStart": 2,
      "pageEnd": 3,
      "target": "../sections/sec-000-1-introduction.md",
      "narrative": null,
      "content": "Richard Feynman described electron diffraction as a phenomenon \"which has in it the heart of quantum mechanics. In reality, it contains the *only* mystery\"[@Feynman65]. He went on to describe a thought experiment for which he stated \"that you should not try to set up\" because \"the apparatus would have to be made on an impossibly small scale to show the effects we are interested in.\" He used these effects to help illustrate the phenomena of wave-particle duality, which is a postulate that all particles exhibit both wave and particle properties. The effects he described were: the relations betwe",
      "truncated": true,
      "provenance": "ingested",
      "summary": {
        "status": "not-summarised"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "library/arxiv-1210.6243v1/blocks/prose-sec-001",
      "types": [
        "folio-assistant-core:Prose",
        "doco:Section"
      ],
      "kind": "prose",
      "title": "Experimental Setup",
      "pageStart": 3,
      "pageEnd": 3,
      "target": "../sections/sec-001-2-experimental-setup.md",
      "narrative": null,
      "content": "The experimental setup is shown diagrammatically in figure [\\[fig1\\]](#fig1){reference-type=\"ref\" reference=\"fig1\"}a. An electron beam with energy of 600 eV was generated with a thermionic tungsten filament and several electrostatic lenses. The beam was collimated with a slit of 2 $\\mu$m width and 10 $\\mu$m height placed at 16.5 cm. The double-slit was located 30.5 cm from the collimation slit. The resulting patterns were magnified by an electrostatic quadrupole lens and imaged on a two-dimensional microchannel plate and phosphorus screen, then recorded with a charge-coupled device camera.\n\nTw",
      "truncated": true,
      "provenance": "ingested",
      "summary": {
        "status": "not-summarised"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "library/arxiv-1210.6243v1/blocks/prose-sec-002",
      "types": [
        "folio-assistant-core:Prose",
        "doco:Section"
      ],
      "kind": "prose",
      "title": "Results",
      "pageStart": 3,
      "pageEnd": 4,
      "target": "../sections/sec-002-3-results.md",
      "narrative": null,
      "content": "The movable mask was placed behind the double-slit, see figure [\\[fig1\\]](#fig1){reference-type=\"ref\" reference=\"fig1\"}. The mask was moved from one side to the other (figure [\\[fig2\\]](#fig2){reference-type=\"ref\" reference=\"fig2\"} top to bottom). Initially the majority of the electrons are blocked. As the mask is moved, slit 1 becomes partially, then fully open. When one slit is open, single-slit diffraction can be observed ($\\textrm{P}_{1}$ in figure [\\[fig2\\]](#fig2){reference-type=\"ref\" reference=\"fig2\"} and figure [\\[fig1\\]](#fig1){reference-type=\"ref\" reference=\"fig1\"}b). Feynman indicat",
      "truncated": true,
      "provenance": "ingested",
      "summary": {
        "status": "not-summarised"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "library/arxiv-1210.6243v1/blocks/prose-sec-003",
      "types": [
        "folio-assistant-core:Prose",
        "doco:Section"
      ],
      "kind": "prose",
      "title": "Conclusion",
      "pageStart": 4,
      "pageEnd": 8,
      "target": "../sections/sec-003-4-conclusion.md",
      "narrative": null,
      "content": "In this paper, we show a full realization of Feynman’s thought experiment and illustrate\nkey features of quatum mechanics: interference and the wave-particle duality of matter.\nBy controlling the transmission through the individual slits of a double-slit we were\nable to observe the diﬀraction patterns from slit 1 (P1), slit 2 (P2), and both (P12), thus\nobserving the wave properties of electrons. Also, by recording single electron detection\nevents diﬀracting through a double-slit we were able to build up a diﬀraction pattern,\nthus observing the particle properties of electrons.\nAcknowledgments",
      "truncated": true,
      "provenance": "ingested",
      "summary": {
        "status": "not-summarised"
      }
    },
